WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.340
Glamorous as it looks, an actor's life is
far from easy.

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:07.640
Though they're rewarded for their work with
cushy homes, luxurious wardrobes, and big

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:11.910
paychecks, performers often make physical
sacrifices for their work that many people

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:12.780
would balk at.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:17.580
Game of Thrones actress Sophie Turner, who
plays Sansa Stark on the fantasy drama series,

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:21.769
has revealed that the HBO gig once required
her to change her real-life hygiene in a way

00:00:21.769 --> 00:00:23.820
she described as "disgusting."

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:28.250
Speaking in an interview with InStyle, Turner
spoke about her character's long locks, and

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:30.019
the way they've changed over the years.

00:00:30.019 --> 00:00:33.910
At the start of the series, when Sansa was
still living a life of privileged royalty,

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:36.040
her hair was kept well-styled and clean.

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:40.320
But as the show progressed, her character's
circumstances got much more dire - a shift

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.220
which was reflected in Sansa's hairstyle.

00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.880
It wasn't all just movie magic - to help sell
the illusion, Turner was instructed to stop

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:51.130
washing her hair in real life, so that it
appeared as dirty, greasy, and grubby as possible

00:00:51.130 --> 00:00:51.860
on the show.

00:00:51.940 --> 00:00:55.680
It's been reported that the actors on Game
of Thrones have had to occasionally quit washing

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.160
their hair in order to make their characters
look suitably medieval.

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:03.050
According to Turner, those reports are true
- and though Sansa's always been pretty far

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:06.150
from the series' battlefields, her character
is no exception.

00:01:06.150 --> 00:01:06.640
She said,

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:10.220
"For the first few seasons I was allowed to
wash my hair because I was an aristocratic

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:11.020
young girl.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:15.900
Towards season 5 they started asking me to
not wash my hair and it was really disgusting.

00:01:15.909 --> 00:01:18.939
Now I wear a wig so I can wash my hair whenever
I want, which is nice.

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:22.220
But yes, for a couple of years I was living
with pretty greasy hair."

00:01:22.369 --> 00:01:26.700
All the build-up of grease and grime, Turner
said, made her scalp uncomfortable and itchy.

00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:31.320
The discomfort was made worse during the series'
more wintry scenes, with snow machines sending

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.060
tiny bits of white paper through the air to
get stuck in her unwashed locks.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:36.240
She said in the interview,

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:39.820
"We would have the snow machines going so
we would have little snow paper particles

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.009
that would get stuck in the grease.

00:01:41.009 --> 00:01:42.000
It was disgusting."

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:43.580
"It can't be any worse."

00:01:44.260 --> 00:01:44.760
"It can.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:47.920
It can always be worse."

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:53.580
A natural blonde, Turner dyed her hair red
to portray Sansa Stark when Game of Thrones

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:54.780
began in 2011.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.850
She kept her hair dyed during the series'
production for years, briefly experimenting

00:01:58.850 --> 00:02:01.850
with black hair during her arc in the custody
of Littlefinger.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:06.220
It wasn't until 2017 that the series finally
allowed her to just wear a wig for the role,

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:10.160
sparing her the trouble of replicating the
character's occasionally grimy look.

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.340
Part of the reason why Game of Thrones has
resonated among so many viewers over the years

00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:18.140
has to do with the attention the show pays
to detail, and the characters' hairstyles

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:19.220
are all a part of that.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:23.400
Sansa's looks have never been arbitrary - they
reflect what's going on in the character's

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:23.900
life.

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:24.900
According to Turner,

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:26.340
"There's definitely a correlation.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:31.320
For so long, Sansa's hair changed season by
season depending on who was influencing her.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:35.280
The first few episodes she was a Northerner
in Winterfell and took after her mother with

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:36.920
a simple braid down the back.

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:41.060
Then when she went to King's Landing it was
a big, decadent halo that was very much like

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:41.980
what the Queen wore.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:44.960
With Littlefinger, she dyes her hair black
and wears all black.

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:47.980
Then she goes back to Winterfell and returns
to her mother's hair."

00:02:47.980 --> 00:02:51.790
The actress has shared that her favorite Sansa
hairstyle came when the character was no longer

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:55.160
being influenced by the people around her,
but instead coming into her own.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.880
As she put it in an interview with Refinery29,

00:02:58.100 --> 00:03:02.200
"Sansa's hair is constantly reflecting the
people she's learning from, or mimicking,

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.620
or inspired by at the time.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:08.120
When she's Cersei's prisoner, and she's kind
of absorbing all her manipulative techniques,

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:12.510
it's reflected in her hairstyle, and when
she's finding the spirit of Margaery in herself,

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.000
she wears her hair like Margaery.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.570
[…] I've always liked when Sansa's hair
was dyed black and pulled back in very tight,

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:18.680
taut French braids.

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:21.400
It was her own look, and something that she'd
created."

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:26.010
Wig or no wig, the climactic final season
of Game of Thrones looks set to be the greasiest

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:26.680
one yet.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:32.000
In a teaser trailer for final episodes, Sansa,
Jon Snow, and Arya Stark can all be seen with

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:36.320
their hair slicked back away from their faces,
a somewhat chic look that Turner says comes

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:37.680
down to chronic neglect.

00:03:37.960 --> 00:03:40.010
The actress has explained the hairstyles,
saying,

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:42.600
"There's been some kind of explosion in Winterfell.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.570
Suddenly, all of our hair just went 'whoop'
and stayed there.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:46.570
The grease really helped.

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:48.050
Actually, it's all just grease.

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:51.470
They haven't washed their hair for so long
that they just brush it back with their hands

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:52.320
and it stays."

00:03:52.480 --> 00:03:56.790
There's been some gruesome, genuinely stomach-churning
stuff on Game of Thrones over the years, from

00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:01.670
Joffrey Baratheon's death by poison to The
Mountain smashing Oberyn Martell's skull.

00:04:01.670 --> 00:04:05.310
But all that stuff is special effects and
make-believe - the realistic, really gross

00:04:05.310 --> 00:04:07.400
hair from a world before modern hygiene?

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:09.920
That's something that actually follows the
actors home.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:11.720
"They find us repulsive."

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:13.420
"I find us repulsive."

